---
source: crates/roxy-rules/tests/diagnostics.rs
expression: out
---
== unknown field
rules[0].when:1:1: unknown field `hots`; did you mean `host`?  [rule a]
    | hots == "x"
    | ^^^^

== type mismatch
rules[0].when:1:1: type mismatch: cannot compare `port` (int) with the string "443"  [rule a]
    | port == "443"
    | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^

== under needs a domain
rules[0].when:1:12: `under` needs a quoted domain on the right, found the number 443  [rule a]
    | host under 443
    |            ^^^

== ordering on strings
rules[0].when:1:1: `<` compares numbers, but this is `path` (string)  [rule a]
    | path < 5
    | ^^^^

== wrong list element type
rules[0].when:1:14: type mismatch: `port` is an int, so the list must contain numbers, but this is the string "443"  [rule a]
    | port in [80, "443"]
    |              ^^^^^

== method literal outside method
rules[0].when:1:9: bare identifier `GET` is only valid as an HTTP method compared with `method`; quote it if you meant the string "GET"  [rule a]
    | path == GET
    |         ^^^

== bare string field as condition
rules[0].when:1:1: `host` is a string, not a condition; compare it with a value, e.g. `host == ...`  [rule a]
    | host and method == GET
    | ^^^^

== not-equal on a list
rules[0].when:1:1: `!=` on a list (`header.all[...]`) is ambiguous; write `not (... == ...)` instead  [rule a]
    | header.all["accept"] != "x"
    | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^

== bad regex
rules[0].when:1:14: invalid regex: unclosed group  [rule a]
    | path matches "(unclosed"
    |              ^^^^^^^^^^^

== bad CIDR mask
rules[0].when:1:15: invalid CIDR `10.0.0.0/33`: prefix length must be at most /32  [rule a]
    | client.ip in [10.0.0.0/33]
    |               ^^^^^^^^^^^

== CIDR with host bits
rules[0].when:1:14: invalid CIDR `192.168.1.1/16`: host bits are set; did you mean `192.168.0.0/16`?  [rule a]
    | client.ip in 192.168.1.1/16
    |              ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^

== unterminated string
rules[0].when:1:9: unterminated string  [rule a]
    | host == "api.github.com
    |         ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^

== allow in a watching rule
rules[0].then[0]: `allow` is only possible in rules decided at the request head; this rule reads `response.status`, which is known only after the request was forwarded (https://roxy-proxy.github.io/roxy-proxy/policies/overview#evaluation). Write the allow as a head rule and this rule as a `deny`  [rule a]

== connect-time fields are gone
rules[0].when:1:1: unknown field `dst.port`  [rule a]
    | dst.port == 22
    | ^^^^^^^^

== unknown metric
rules[0].when:1:23: reference to undefined metric `metric.nope` (define it under `metrics`)  [rule a]
    | metric.writes > 3 and metric.nope > 1
    |                       ^^^^^^^^^^^

== multi-line expression
rules[0].when:3:17: `like` needs a quoted glob pattern on the right, found the number 5  [rule a]
    |   and path like 5
    |                 ^

== unknown secret
rules[0].then[0]: reference to undefined secret "missing" (define it under `secrets`)  [rule a]

== secret in a watching rule
rules[0].then[0]: secret references are only allowed in rules decided at the request head (they set request headers); this rule watches `response.status`  [rule a]

== secret outside set_header
rules[0].then[0]: secret references are only allowed in `set_header` values and the credentials of `sign`, not in `log`  [rule a]

== sign unsigned_payload outside S3
rules[0].then[0]: sign aws_sigv4: `unsigned_payload` is accepted only for S3 (`s3`, `s3-control`, `s3-outposts`); "bedrock" requires the payload hash  [rule a]

== sign with undefined and misplaced secrets, and an empty credential
rules[0].then[0]: secret references are only allowed in `set_header` values and the credentials of `sign`, not in the `service` or `region` of `sign`  [rule a]
rules[0].then[0]: reference to undefined secret "missing" (define it under `secrets`)  [rule a]
rules[0].then[0]: sign aws_sigv4: `secret_access_key` must not be empty  [rule a]

== sign in a watching rule
rules[0].then[0]: `sign` changes the request, which is already on its way; this rule reads `response.status`, which is known only after the request was forwarded (https://roxy-proxy.github.io/roxy-proxy/policies/overview#evaluation)  [rule a]

== passthrough is reserved
parse: .[0].then: `passthrough` is reserved for a transparent listener, which roxy does not have (issue #15) at line 1 column 18

== call is reserved
parse: .[0].then[0]: `call` is reserved: addons run above the rules, in config order, not from a rule at line 1 column 19

== tag read above the rule that sets it
rules[0].when: reads `tag["risky"]`, which is set too late to be seen here, by rules[1] ("mark"), below this rule; a rule sees only the tags set before it is checked  [rule read]

== tag read at the head but set by a watching rule
rules[1].when: reads `tag["big"]`, which is set too late to be seen here, by rules[0] ("big"), a watching rule, which fires when the values it reads arrive rather than in list order; a rule sees only the tags set before it is checked  [rule read]

== tag read by a watching rule but set by a watching rule above it
rules[1].when: reads `tag["big"]`, which is set too late to be seen here, by rules[0] ("big"), a watching rule, which fires when the values it reads arrive rather than in list order; a rule sees only the tags set before it is checked  [rule cap]

== rewrite_path group references
rules[0].then[0]: rewrite_path `to`: `$3` refers to a group `match` does not have (1 unnamed group; named: rest)  [rule a]
rules[0].then[0]: rewrite_path `to`: `$nope` refers to a group `match` does not have (1 unnamed group; named: rest)  [rule a]
rules[0].then[0]: rewrite_path `to`: `$1a` refers to a group `match` does not have; a reference runs to the end of the word, write `${1}a`  [rule a]

== metric where reads a tag
metrics[0].where: a metric's `where` cannot read tags: tags are set by rules and addons on each flow, and whether a flow counts is decided without them; it reads `tag["billing"]`

== multi-key action map
parse: .[0].then: an action is a map with exactly one key, but this one has `set_header` and `allow`; write each action as its own list item at line 1 column 18

== terminal action followed by another
rules[0].then[1]: `tag` follows the terminal action `allow` and would never run; move it before `allow`  [rule a]

== unknown action
parse: .[0].then: unknown action `sethead`; expected one of `allow`, `deny`, `set_header`, `remove_header`, `rewrite_path`, `set_query`, `remove_query`, `redirect`, `tag`, `log`, `set_state`, `capture`, `sign` at line 1 column 18

== request mutation in a watching rule
rules[0].then[0]: `rewrite_path` changes the request, which is already on its way; this rule reads `body.bytes`, which is known only after the request was forwarded (https://roxy-proxy.github.io/roxy-proxy/policies/overview#evaluation)  [rule a]

== request header change in a watching rule
rules[0].then[0]: `set_header` in this rule would change the request, which is already on its way; this rule reads `body.bytes`, which is known only after the request was forwarded (https://roxy-proxy.github.io/roxy-proxy/policies/overview#evaluation). (In a rule that reads response values it changes the response.)  [rule a]

== response header change that could fire after the head is sent
rules[0].then[0]: `remove_header` changes the response head, so every value the rule reads must be known before the response head is sent; this rule also reads response.body.bytes, which can change after that  [rule a]

== reserved header and bad value
rules[0].then[0]: header `host` is managed by roxy and cannot be changed by rules (use `redirect: { ..., rewrite_host: true }`)  [rule a]
rules[0].then[1]: set_header x-a: value "café" is not a valid header value (visible ASCII, space and tab only)  [rule a]

== bad deny status
rules[0].then[0]: deny status 200 must be a 4xx or 5xx code  [rule a]

== duplicate and reserved ids
rules[1].id: duplicate rule id "a" (first defined at rules[0])  [rule a]
rules[2].id: rule id "_default": ids starting with `_` are reserved (`_default`, `_fail_closed`)  [rule _default]
rules[3].id: rule id "_fail_closed": ids starting with `_` are reserved (`_default`, `_fail_closed`)  [rule _fail_closed]

== address list on a non-ip field
rules[0].when:1:9: address list @internal can only be used with `in`/`not in` on an ip field  [rule a]
    | host in @internal
    |         ^^^^^^^^^

== address list with ==
rules[0].when:1:1: address list @internal can only be used with `in`/`not in` on an ip field  [rule a]
    | @internal == 1
    | ^^^^^^^^^

== address list inside a list literal
rules[0].when:1:27: address list @internal can only be used with `in`/`not in` on an ip field  [rule a]
    | client.ip in [10.0.0.0/8, @internal]
    |                           ^^^^^^^^^

== address list as a condition
rules[0].when:1:1: address list @internal can only be used with `in`/`not in` on an ip field  [rule a]
    | @internal
    | ^^^^^^^^^

== undefined address list
rules[0].when:1:18: reference to undefined address list `@nope` (define it under `address_lists`)  [rule a]
    | client.ip not in @nope
    |                  ^^^^^

== nullable metric key without a guard
metrics[0].key[1]: `tls.sni` can be null, and a null key field denies the flow; guard it with `where: tls.sni != null` (or `tls.sni != null and ...`) so that such flows are not counted (https://roxy-proxy.github.io/roxy-proxy/policies/rate-limits#metrics)
metrics[1].count: `body.size` can be null, and a null unique() field denies the flow; guard it with `where: body.size != null` (or `body.size != null and ...`) so that such flows are not counted (https://roxy-proxy.github.io/roxy-proxy/policies/rate-limits#metrics)
metrics[2].key[0]: `query.raw` can be null, and a null key field denies the flow; guard it with `where: query.raw != null` (or `query.raw != null and ...`) so that such flows are not counted (https://roxy-proxy.github.io/roxy-proxy/policies/rate-limits#metrics)
metrics[3].key[0]: `tls.alpn` can be null, and a null key field denies the flow; guard it with `where: tls.alpn != null` (or `tls.alpn != null and ...`) so that such flows are not counted (https://roxy-proxy.github.io/roxy-proxy/policies/rate-limits#metrics)

== bad metrics
metrics[0].id: invalid metric id "bad-id": must match [A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]* so it can be used as metric.<id>
metrics[0].window: window must be greater than zero
metrics[0].max_keys: must be at least 1
metrics[0].key[0]: unknown key field `client.nope`; metric fields must be scalar fields such as client.ip, host, tls.sni
metrics[1].where:1:1: unknown field `dst.port`
    | dst.port == 1
    | ^^^^^^^^
metrics[2].count: unknown unique() field `nope`; metric fields must be scalar fields such as client.ip, host, tls.sni
metrics[3].where: a metric's `where` may only read head fields, because whether an exchange counts is decided at the request head; it reads `response.status` (https://roxy-proxy.github.io/roxy-proxy/policies/rate-limits#metrics)
metrics[3].key[0]: `body.bytes` is known only after forwarding; metric key fields must be head fields (https://roxy-proxy.github.io/roxy-proxy/policies/rate-limits#metrics)
metrics[4].count: `response.status` is known only after forwarding; metric unique() fields must be head fields (https://roxy-proxy.github.io/roxy-proxy/policies/rate-limits#metrics)
